      "title": "Two methods answer different questions",
      "narration": "The human-life-value approach looks at the financial value of future earnings or contributions. A detailed estimate uses expected working years and present-value assumptions, with appropriate adjustments for amounts not supporting survivors. The needs approach starts with the specific money the survivors would require, then subtracts resources available to meet those needs. These methods can produce different estimates because their assumptions differ. Neither number measures a person's worth. The purpose is to estimate a financial exposure. Make the assumptions visible and review them as the household's circumstances change.",
